p.src:
For a very low-energy excitation the field remains close to $\rho=f$. Then $\mathcal L_{\rm angular}
  \simeq f^2(\partial_\mu\alpha)(\partial^\mu\alpha).$ Define the canonically normalized angular field, up to the convention used for factors of $1/2$, by $\beta=\sqrt{2}\,f\alpha,
  \qquad
  \mathcal L_{\rm angular}
  =\frac12(\partial_\mu\beta)(\partial^\mu\beta).$ There is no $\beta^2$ term. The quanta of this angular field are massless. They are the Goldstone bosons.

